# IP Addressing and Routing: Finding Every Machine on Earth

> Every device on the Internet needs an address, and every packet needs
> a path to one. This lesson covers IPv4 structure, subnetting, private
> ranges, IPv6, and the protocols that choose routes.

## 1. IPv4: 32 Bits in Dotted Decimal

An **IPv4 address** is 32 bits written as four **octets** in dotted
decimal, each ranging 0–255: 192.168.10.42. That is about 4.3 billion
addresses — the pool that ran out, and the reason IPv6 exists.

[definition]
Every IPv4 address splits into a **network part** (which network) and
a **host part** (which machine on it). The **subnet mask** draws the
line: 255.255.255.0, written /24, means the first 24 bits are network.
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="ipv4-split" layout="tree" aria="An IPv4 address split by its slash 24 mask into network and host parts"
  "192.168.10.42 /24" -> "Network: 192.168.10"
  "192.168.10.42 /24" -> "Host: .42"
@/diagram

## 2. Classful Legacy and CIDR

The original scheme split addresses into fixed classes:

| Class | Leading bits | First octet | Default mask | Typical size |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A | 0 | 1–126 | /8 | ~16.7M hosts |
| B | 10 | 128–191 | /16 | 65,534 hosts |
| C | 110 | 192–223 | /24 | 254 hosts |
| D | 1110 | 224–239 | — | multicast |
| E | 1111 | 240–255 | — | reserved |

Rigid classes wasted addresses by the million, so **CIDR**
(Classless Inter-Domain Routing) replaced them in 1993: any prefix
length is allowed, matched to actual need.

## 3. Subnetting: Borrowing Bits

Subnetting steals bits from the host part to create smaller networks.
Splitting 192.168.10.0/24 into /26 blocks:

| Subnet | Usable range | Hosts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 192.168.10.0/26 | .1 – .62 | 62 |
| 192.168.10.64/26 | .65 – .126 | 62 |
| 192.168.10.128/26 | .129 – .190 | 62 |
| 192.168.10.192/26 | .193 – .254 | 62 |

Each block holds 64 addresses; the first (the network address) and the
last (broadcast) are reserved, leaving 62 usable. A /30 — 4 addresses,
2 usable — is the classic point-to-point link mask.

| Prefix | Total addresses | Usable hosts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| /24 | 256 | 254 |
| /25 | 128 | 126 |
| /26 | 64 | 62 |
| /28 | 16 | 14 |
| /30 | 4 | 2 |

## 4. Private Ranges and NAT

| Private range | Block | Usual home |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 10.0.0.0/8 | 10.x.x.x | large enterprises |
| 172.16.0.0/12 | 172.16 – 172.31 | mid-size networks |
| 192.168.0.0/16 | 192.168.x.x | homes and small offices |

These are not routable on the public Internet. **NAT** (Network
Address Translation) on the router rewrites private source addresses
to its one public address — tracking which port belongs to which
machine — so a whole household shares a single public IP.

## 5. Special Addresses Worth Knowing

| Address | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 127.0.0.1 | loopback — this machine itself (localhost) |
| 0.0.0.0 | this network / unspecified source |
| 169.254.0.0/16 | link-local (APIPA) — self-assigned, DHCP failed |
| 255.255.255.255 | broadcast to everyone on this segment |

[warning]
Seeing a 169.254.x.x address on a laptop almost always means the DHCP
server was unreachable — check the cable or the Wi-Fi before blaming
the operating system.
[/warning]

## 6. IPv6: The 128-Bit Answer

**IPv6** widens the address to 128 bits, written as eight hex groups:
2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:1 shortens to 2001:db8::1 by dropping leading zeros
and collapsing one run of zeros. About 3.4 × 10³⁸ addresses exist —
enough that every device can be globally reachable without NAT.

| IPv4 habit | IPv6 change |
| --- | --- |
| NAT to survive address shortage | global addresses for every device |
| broadcast | multicast (to a group) and anycast (to nearest of a group) |
| manual or DHCP setup | SLAAC autoconfiguration, DHCPv6 optional |
| ARP for neighbour discovery | built-in neighbour discovery protocol |

## 7. Routing: Static and Dynamic

| Protocol | Family | Metric / idea | Home |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| RIP | distance-vector | hop count, max 15 | small networks |
| OSPF | link-state | cost from bandwidth; runs Dijkstra | inside one AS |
| BGP | path-vector | policy-weighted paths between ASes | the Internet backbone |

- **Static routes** are hand-written — predictable, zero overhead, and
  no self-healing when a link dies.
- **Dynamic protocols** exchange reachability and re-route around
  failure automatically.
- `traceroute` reveals the actual hop-by-hop path packets take.

## 8. A Word on Names

Numbers route packets; names route people. Before the first packet
leaves, DNS turns example.com into an address:

@diagram tree id="dns-resolution-chain" layout="tree" aria="DNS resolution walking from root servers to the TLD then the authoritative name server"
  "Resolver asks a root server" -> "Root: ask the .com TLD servers"
  "Root: ask the .com TLD servers" -> "TLD: ask example.com's servers"
  "TLD: ask example.com's servers" -> "Answer: 93.184.216.34"
@/diagram

Record types, caching, and the hosts file are covered in the
application-protocols lesson.

[summary]
IPv4 is 32 bits split by mask into network and host parts; CIDR freed
prefix lengths from classes; a /26 subnet holds 62 usable hosts.
Private ranges 10.x, 172.16–31, 192.168.x hide behind NAT. IPv6 brings
128 bits, no broadcast, no NAT. RIP, OSPF, BGP route at increasing
scales — BGP glues the Internet together.
[/summary]
